<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Lower California</h1><p class="st-meta">1853 CE – 1854 CE · capital La Paz; later Ensenada</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Paper republic proclaimed by the American filibuster William Walker after his raid on Baja California in 1853, which collapsed within months.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a>, a Tennessee-born adventurer, sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> with about 45 men and landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz-baja-california-sur">La Paz</a> in November 1853. He arrested the governor and declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baja-california">Baja California</a> an independent republic with himself as president, then moved north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ensenada">Ensenada</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic existed mainly on paper. In January 1854 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">Walker</a> also proclaimed the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a>, claiming the mainland state, and introduced the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. His men had little food or money, many deserted, and Mexican forces and local ranchers harassed them. He controlled only a few settlements on the Pacific coast.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">Walker</a>'s attempt to march into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a> failed, and in May 1854 he and his remaining men surrendered to US forces at the border. He was tried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> for violating neutrality laws and acquitted. He later seized power in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> and was executed in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Honduras</a> in 1860.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-lower-california.jpg" alt="Republic of Lower California" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Heraldry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_Lower_California.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1853 CE – 1854 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>La Paz; later Ensenada</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William Walker</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Sonora</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=republic-of-lower-california" aria-label="Republic of Lower California on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1745 1288 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Lower California lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2065 1544h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2065 1544h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks La Paz. <a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=republic-of-lower-california">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

